1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,420
Proposition thirty-seven creates a loan program for buyers of qualifying new

2
00:00:04,420 --> 00:00:09,180
homes. The problem it targets is the upfront cash needed to buy, not the

3
00:00:09,180 --> 00:00:14,580
entire cost of housing. Assistance would be another loan, not free money.

4
00:00:14,580 --> 00:00:18,130
The state housing finance agency could issue up to twenty-five billion

5
00:00:18,130 --> 00:00:23,020
dollars in revenue bonds. Homebuyers’ payments would repay investors and

6
00:00:23,020 --> 00:00:27,960
administration. The analyst finds no direct state or local cost under this

7
00:00:27,960 --> 00:00:28,320
structure.

8
00:00:28,933 --> 00:00:33,833
Imagine a family budgeting for a newly built home. For an illustrative home

9
00:00:33,833 --> 00:00:37,973
costing six hundred thousand dollars, three percent down is eighteen

10
00:00:37,973 --> 00:00:44,049
thousand dollars. A loan covering seventeen percent would be one hundred two

11
00:00:44,049 --> 00:00:48,333
thousand. The remaining eighty percent is four hundred eighty thousand. That

12
00:00:48,333 --> 00:00:52,533
can reduce cash needed at closing, but the family still owes the assistance

13
00:00:52,533 --> 00:00:57,393
loan as well as its main mortgage. Affordability depends on both monthly

14
00:00:57,393 --> 00:01:03,833
payments, interest rates, taxes, insurance, and other costs. The program is

15
00:01:03,833 --> 00:01:05,913
tied to qualifying new housing.

16
00:01:06,650 --> 00:01:11,420
Borrowers must meet one year of California residency, owner-occupancy, and

17
00:01:11,420 --> 00:01:16,560
income rules, including an income ceiling tied to twice the area’s median.

18
00:01:16,560 --> 00:01:21,060
Homes must be qualifying first sales, with county-based price limits roughly

19
00:01:21,060 --> 00:01:26,140
around one million to one and a half million dollars, adjusted annually. How

20
00:01:26,140 --> 00:01:30,500
many loans could it fund? Using the example loan amount, the bond could

21
00:01:30,500 --> 00:01:34,640
support roughly two hundred and forty-five thousand loans. That is

22
00:01:34,640 --> 00:01:40,260
arithmetic, not an official forecast; actual loan sizes, issuance, and costs

23
00:01:40,260 --> 00:01:45,200
matter. Developers can build eligible homes without using the optional

24
00:01:45,200 --> 00:01:49,620
qualified-builder route. That option pairs higher labor standards with

25
00:01:49,620 --> 00:01:54,080
different construction-defect rules. The measure does not directly guarantee

26
00:01:54,080 --> 00:01:56,560
