27
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:01,700
A voter might support helping households with adequate income but too little

28
00:02:01,700 --> 00:02:06,200
saved cash. Restricting the assistance to new homes could encourage

29
00:02:06,200 --> 00:02:10,820
construction, connecting stronger purchasing power to additional supply.

30
00:02:10,820 --> 00:02:14,080
Whether developers respond enough is uncertain.

31
00:02:14,733 --> 00:02:19,793
You might ask whether easier buying pushes prices up. If more buyers can bid

32
00:02:19,793 --> 00:02:24,033
while construction stays constrained, some benefit can be absorbed into

33
00:02:24,033 --> 00:02:29,653
higher prices. New-home eligibility helps link the program to supply, but

34
00:02:29,653 --> 00:02:34,613
does not remove land, labor, or permitting constraints. Easier financing

35
00:02:34,613 --> 00:02:40,273
alone is not a complete affordability solution. A skeptical voter could also

36
00:02:40,273 --> 00:02:45,473
question the extra household debt or the construction-defect tradeoff. No

37
00:02:45,473 --> 00:02:50,373
direct government cost does not mean every borrower benefits, or that every

38
00:02:50,373 --> 00:02:51,453
risk disappears.

39
00:02:52,067 --> 00:02:57,727
In the September PPIC survey of likely voters, 61 percent said yes, 35

40
